00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.860
There's a concept I feel has died as of late within our community. One that used to be a

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:11.180
standard part of getting into the medium and that is the idea around classic anime.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.420
Anime that define their generation and pioneered the landscape you see today.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.420
It used to be mandatory to at least go back and watch a handful of them,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.920
but nowadays, not only is there less obligation to get yourself cultured by watching these shows,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:30.720
I also kind of feel like no matter how great any new anime coming out is, there's nothing that really sticks out to me that we could call a modern classic.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.320
I off handedly mentioned in my Best of Anime 2018 video that despite how great I thought last year was,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:43.180
it had zero classics, which was more my way of describing that no matter how good any show was, it seemed to lack a sense of impact and longevity

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.740
that a lot of greater years of anime had. But it certainly sparked an unintentional debate in the comments section

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.360
about what exactly a classic even is and whether it's too early to judge.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:59.460
So today, I wanted to really dig in on that exact topic, what's lacking today, and really remind everyone why they were

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.500
important in the first place. There's a lot of different things I want to unpack in this video,

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.200
so without further ado, let's just jump into it.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:06.840
What

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:07.360
What makes

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.720
What makes a classic?

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:09.320
What makes a Classic FM? 
(Yay! A British joke no one gets!)

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:15.680
I don't think I need to state that the exact parameters of what defines a classic is entirely subjective and probably different for every

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:16.860
person.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.800
But I guess I just did anyway.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:26.220
Most common definitions of the word, in a broader sense, simply played out to be something of universally recognized quality and established ongoing value.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.820
However, for the sake of the argument I'm making, for me it boils down to three main points...

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:32.780
Number One:

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.020
Number One: Staying Power

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.880
Classics are classics because they have stood the test of time. No matter how many years pass,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.830
they will still be relevant in their own way.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:45.420
There are many older anime that have aged in some way, mainly to do with their art style or

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.980
animation, but have preserved a sense of timelessness, with either their story or

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:55.980
characters, that can be enjoyed in any era. As a side note, that have been many shows that set trends and pioneered legendary tropes on

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.640
the bleeding edge of what was fresh and

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:03.560
significant in the time they came out, but have lost a lot of their luster through these elements becoming oversaturated years later.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.460
The Vision of Escaflowne, for example, pioneered a lot of things, and there was a time

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.160
it was considered a classic and I'm sure many still do. Now though,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.020
it's a lot harder to recommend for newer audiences, as most people who watch it for the first time could see it as some

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.420
generic, isekai-mecha show, because of how

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:24.029
overplayed many of its tropes have become since, and, in my opinion, no matter how revolutionary a show is,

00:03:24.029 --> 00:03:28.420
this is a test it has to overcome. Which brings me to point Number Two:

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:29.540
Impact 
(Presumably not like Eva)

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.020
I feel there needs to be a level of impact and influence

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.360
because logically, it must have been able to impact its own time in order for it to be remembered.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.180
And this goes in two ways: an impact on the anime industry

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:46.020
itself that influences future titles and the landscape of anime, or an impact on anime pop culture and the general audience of the time.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:50.939
I don't think I need to clarify what kind of impact anime like Neon Genesis Evangelion or Akira had,

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.420
but I could talk about more recent anime that had a profound impact on anime pop culture.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:01.360
If I shouted the phrase "And this is to go even further beyond!" or "Just who the hell do you think I am?",

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.460
how many of you would immediately know what I'm talking about? Some classics

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:11.879
I feel only have an impact on the industry, some on pop culture, and some doing both. But I think it's important because if an

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:19.580
anime hits every other category, but isn't that popular or widely influencial, in my opinion, it's closer to a cult classic. And finally,

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:20.780
Number Three:

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:21.480
Number Three: Quality.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.140
The term classic surely implies that there would be a certain level of quality or critical acclaim behind it.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:31.850
You can have something that's remained relevant for years and is also widely influential, as you would see with a lot of gateway anime,

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:38.440
but unless there's a general agreed consensus that it's up to a certain level of high standards, people won't take it seriously.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.460
I think the best example of this is the Sword Art Online franchise, which is still way more relevant than arguably

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:51.060
any other anime that came out in the same year, and undoubtedly has had the popularity to back it up while influencing the landscape of modern anime.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.920
But would anyone actually call this a classic?

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:02.540
Now with these points in mind, how many, if any, could you apply all three criteria to an anime today?

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.020
To me,

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:12.170
it's the feeling of a modern classic that's been missing from anime recently, and I don't want you to get the impression that that means

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.280
anime has gotten worse. Quite the opposite in fact

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.640
I think it's better than has ever been, and that might actually be it. Never before has such a wide variety of high-quality

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.860
anime been available to us in a constant drip feed of seasonal content, never giving us a moment's rest.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:31.120
We've been spoilt for choice, and I think it kind of shows when I think this spring season is kind of weak just because it only has

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:37.969
two shows I'm super hyped for and five shows I think are pretty good. Get out of here. What do you mean

00:05:37.969 --> 00:05:43.480
I'm not literally spending every free moment trying to keep up with shows worth watching like the last two seasons?

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:44.660
(Sᴜʙsᴄʀɪʙᴇ ᴛᴏ CᴀʟʟMᴇCᴀʀsᴏɴ)

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:52.680
While there are less shows pioneering new things, which is understandable considering how old this medium is right now,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.209
I think the average quality and very certainly the quantity of choice has gone up.

00:05:57.209 --> 00:06:01.360
But that's the thing, if everything is special, nothing is.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.720
Of all the three criteria, it's Staying Power

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.640
that seems to be missing most of the time.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.900
Current trends and hype series cover the majority of what remains relevant and impactful throughout the wider community,

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:17.280
with the sheer density of new series being fed to us every season, it can become easy to forget even great shows.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.740
We used to complain that anime used to have filler seasons. Now, it feels like we have filler anime. You could

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:28.280
argue that it's far too early to determine which recently aired anime has staying power

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:33.260
But I'd argue that you'd be hard-pressed to find any show that lingers around for longer than three months,

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.540
let alone years or decades later. Just because not a lot of time has passed

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.280
doesn't mean you can't have a good idea of what will stick around.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:45.860
There have been plenty of sporting events that have been labeled an instant classic, as soon as the last episode of Breaking Bad aired

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:51.090
I knew was gonna go down in television history, and with what Infinity War and Endgame have achieved and how much is

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:58.169
permeated into general pop culture, I have no doubt they will become classics that have defined this generation. On a rare occurrence, though

00:06:58.169 --> 00:07:03.820
I will watch an anime that does something special that would stay in my mind for a very long time, even in this climate.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.780
Something that goes above and beyond even compared to the best and has that special

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.700
X-Factor you rarely see. The anime that I would say has come closest to becoming a classic recently is

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:15.820
Mob Psycho 100 - Season 2.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:20.220
Hell, I know many of you would probably argue it already is, and I completely get that.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:26.120
Season 2 of Mob Psycho is one of the best anime series I've seen in years, that completely blew the first season out of the water.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:30.420
It somehow leveled up its orgasmic animation with some of the best fight scenes of the last decade,

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.260
and paired this with a heart and intimate character writing that wasn't as

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:40.860
prevalent before, while ending every major plot thread it had set up, resulting in an all-round package that will certainly stick with me for years to come.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.980
It was just such a perfect storm of greatness on every level that you rarely get to see.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.360
So what exactly is it missing?

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.400
Well, it's precisely because...

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:56.489
it's a season two. As much of a masterpiece as I think it was a lot of people were dropped off after season 1,

00:07:56.489 --> 00:08:01.800
which is to be expected in the fast-paced seasonal anime cycle and an over two year gap between seasons.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.820
Even I had trouble remembering everything the first season left off at, and I'm sure this killed off a lot of the potential

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:13.080
wide-scale impact it could have had. I have no doubts that if it was one long season, or even if the gap was shorter,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:19.080
It would have permeated the community much deeper than it did, but as it stands it wasn't even the most popular show of its own

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.100
season. There was more discussion around Shield Hero. More fresh hype around Promised Neverland.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:27.780
And more memes around Kaguya-Sama. This, I think, perfectly illustrates

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:32.400
why it's so hard for anime today to get a sense of longevity. Right now, Anime has become too

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.580
segmented to have the same influence, and the community moves too fast to have the same staying power it used to.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:40.660
I'm sure it's still possible, it's just much harder.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:44.549
It has to be able to stand out from the dozens of anime it competes with every season.

00:08:44.549 --> 00:08:49.660
It has to be given a long enough run time to have a lasting impact, without big breaks if it's broken up into seasons.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:54.060
It has to be easily accessible on a streaming platform that the community will collectively access,

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.600
and, on top of that, it has to be...

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:57.340
you know....

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:57.840
Really

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:58.340
Really Fucking

00:08:58.340 --> 00:08:58.840
Really Fucking Good.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.676
I do, however, think there's a small asterisk to this point,

00:09:01.676 --> 00:09:03.400
as I think every generation will have a

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:10.169
long-running shonen that defines that era and goes down as a classic. With it being a staple of gateway anime and it's long-running nature

00:09:10.169 --> 00:09:16.340
allows it far more time to build the popularity and impact and staying power because, well, it sticks around for so long

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:21.080
So if there is an exception to everything, I've said here it would, of course, be:

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:22.620
My Hero Academia

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.620
I do think this is the one show that has done enough to be remembered for a long time to come, but I also consider

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:32.910
these types of shows to play by a different set of rules than the Mob Psychos of the anime world. Because part of the reason

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:38.300
the idea of classics has died is because, well, we don't feel the need to look back into the past anymore.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:42.240
Even if the past is just a year or two ago. There's so much to choose from now,

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.660
What's the point of looking back?

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:49.000
And that's the real reason I wanted to make this video. Because over the past few months,

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.180
I actually did set a bit of time to watch some classic anime I had never seen before.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:58.400
And it just reminded me why classics are indeed called classics.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:03.660
Two months ago

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:09.040
I set off on an arduous mission to start the anime that had been sitting on my watch list for years.

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:12.760
Waiting until I was ready to take that leap of faith to finally watch it.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.720
Legend of the Galactic Heroes.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.700
It was an undertaking that was as intimidating as the reputation that

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:25.640
preceded it. A classic so highly spoken of, it has literally been memed to death by many people, myself included, as the one show that's been

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:27.400
put up on the highest pedestal.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:31.580
"Anime won't be the same once you watch Legends of the Galactic Heroes"

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:38.760
they said, and with this all in mind, what I found was exactly what I had expected.

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:43.600
One of the greatest stories to have been told in the history of anime.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.520
A tale so grand, no other anime even comes close to the scale,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:53.540
depth, and density of the saga told here. A production that took almost ten years to complete, contained in a whopping

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:58.760
110 episodes for a one-of-a-kind experience you can't find anywhere else in this medium.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:02.609
It's not the kind of fast-paced instant gratification anime more common today,

00:11:02.609 --> 00:11:06.660
but a full-length epic you gradually immerse yourself into.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.340
With a show like this you can get so caught up in its reputation

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:16.260
that it can be easy to forget how and why it got its reputation in the first place. Through this, it reminded me

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:20.560
why there used to be this big notion that you should go out and watch the classics.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:22.160
Not just because it was a great show,

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:28.800
there are plenty of great shows that are coming out right now, but because it's a unique experience from anything you can find today.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:34.320
There's a reason the classics have survived the passage of time. They might have pioneered something,

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:37.409
they might have left a deep impression on the audience of their time,

00:11:37.409 --> 00:11:44.780
but most importantly, it's because throughout the years that came after, nothing came along that was able to replace them.

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:50.400
I can safely say since I first watched Cowboy Bebop, there hasn't been any other show quite like it .

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:57.500
Serial Experiments Lain has a depiction of technology that somehow seems more relevant today than when it came out in 1998.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:01.280
Akira still looks better than 90% of the anime coming out now.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:05.960
Neon Genesis Evangelion redefines what it means to be genre defining.

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:11.320
It can be easy to forget through the current sea of Isekai Power Fantasy Highschool rom-coms of today

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.100
that anime didn't always look like this.

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:20.169
So if you think this season is a little slow and you have some time to fill, just pick a classic title that's been collecting dust

00:12:20.169 --> 00:12:24.020
in your watch list and I guarantee, it'll be a way more fulfilling

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:28.420
experience than watching another disposable show that's been airing in the last year.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.980
Whether or not you agree on which anime counts as classics, or even

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:34.800
if you disagree with my opinion that we haven't gotten any recently,

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:38.940
I think it's important to keep the idea of classics alive, because it does feel like lately

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:44.120
there's been a depreciation in the value of longevity and revisiting the past.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:49.940
So if there's anything I wanted to do with this video, it's reminding people that there is a reason to look back

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:54.400
It's taking a break from the current climate and having a look at a product of another time.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:59.560
It's having that realization of "Oh, this is what everyone was talking about."

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.820
It's experiencing a show that has done something to deserve to be remembered.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:09.340
Because one of the saddest things I think could happen to classics is for them to be

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:11.340
forgotten.

